[{:db/id -1,
  :adr/id "2607244600",
  :adr/title "cloud-itonami-commitment-ledger + cloud-itonami-isic-6492: kotobase.net persistence migration (KV -> langchain.kotoba-db :db-api injection), numeric-identity-attr fix, 2 upstream langchain.kotoba-db bugs, 3 confirmed-live CACAO wire-format bugs",
  :adr/status :accepted,
  :adr/date "2026-07-24",
  :adr/doc_type "adr",
  :adr/source-format "edn",
  :adr/related ["2607184000-kotobase-net-tenant-graph-read-acl-and-cloud-itonami-5820-store-backend"
                "2607242200-cloud-itonami-isic6492-commitledger-cross-actor-wiring"
                "2607241800-cloud-itonami-commitment-ledger-actor-blueprint"
                "cloud-itonami-commitment-ledger/docs/adr/0004-kotobase-persistence-migration.md"
                "cloud-itonami-isic-6492/docs/adr/0003-kotobase-persistence-migration.md"],
  :adr/scope-and-design "Both actors' persistence layer switched from a hand-rolled Cloudflare KV JSON blob (commitledger.edge.kv-store / credit.edge.kv-store) to real kotobase.net persistence, via langchain.kotoba-db's :db-api injection into each repo's previously-unwired DatomicStore (commitledger.store / credit.store), following cloud-itonami-isic-5820's crm.store/store-with-api + crm.kotobase pattern (ADR-2607184000) as the reference precedent. DatomicStore in both repos gained an injectable :db-api field (store-with-api) + a small set of chain-aware methods (a helper tolerating either a plain synchronous value or, under :cljs, a real js/Promise) -- ONLY those methods are ever called against the remote/async db-api; every other Store method still assumes synchronous access and is only ever invoked against the in-process snapshot store the unmodified StateGraph runs against per request (langgraph.graph/run* executes fully synchronously; a Cloudflare Pages Function has no synchronous I/O primitive at all, so nothing mid-graph can be a real network call -- this constraint, not present in isic-5820's own JVM-hosted integration, is the one structural adaptation both these repos needed beyond the reference precedent). Both repos' KV persistence is replaced by implementing each repo's OWN EXISTING KVStore protocol (KotobaseKVStore) against kotobase.net, rather than inventing a new boundary -- this meant the existing kv-store.cljc's load-store!/save-store!, and every *-core! handler and existing test driving them via a mem-kv-store fixture, needed ZERO changes; only the bottom-of-file on-request-* Cloudflare Pages Function entry points swap the KV constructor for the kotobase one. kv_store.cljc is left in place, unused in production, in both repos, per this workspace's don't-delete-until-certain convention.",
  :adr/numeric-identity-attr-fix "REQUIRED correctness fix, independently confirmed in both repos' schemas before this migration began: kotobase-server's do-transact collides EVERY :db.unique/identity attribute whose value is a NUMBER (not a string) into entity id \"\" (empty string), silently merging every such entry into ONE entity -- a known, unresolved issue recorded in ADR-2607184000's own known-issues section. commitledger.store marks 3 such attrs identity, all previously (count ...) ints: :ledger/seq, :commitment/seq, :tranche/seq. credit.store marks 2: :ledger/seq, :disbursement/seq. Fixed identically in both: every write site now wraps the value in str; every numeric-sort read site (sort-by first on the [seq fact] pairs) now decodes via a new parse-seq-num fn first (sort-by (comp parse-seq-num first)), since sort-by first on now-string values would silently sort lexicographically once a count reaches double digits. Regression-tested in both repos (store_numeric_identity_test.cljc) -- and, critically, verified to actually FAIL before the fix: the fix was temporarily reverted in commitment-ledger, the test suite rerun, 3 assertions failed exactly as expected (values like 0/1/2 as raw Integers instead of strings), then the fix was re-applied and the suite rerun clean -- proving the test genuinely catches the bug rather than merely asserting the code as-written.",
  :adr/upstream-langchain-kotoba-db-bugs-found-and-fixed "TWO genuine bugs found and fixed in kotoba-lang/langchain's langchain.kotoba-db.cljc (a shared library, out of scope to modify per this task's own boundary UNLESS a genuine currently-unfixed bug blocks the work -- both qualify, both are additive/non-breaking, both are covered by new regression tests, the full existing suite of 66 tests/165 assertions still passes plus the new ones, 74 tests/179 assertions total). (1) decode-pull-value (used by BOTH the pre-existing synchronous kotoba-api and the new kotoba-api-async, see below) over-eagerly auto-decoded an already-pr-str-encoded compound BLOB value (e.g. :lender/:personal-pledge, stored pr-str'd by every consuming store's own enc/ls-enc convention specifically so this substrate treats it as an opaque string) into a live map/vector, corrupting callers (pull->application etc) that expect to decode the blob exactly once themselves -- confirmed directly: a mocked :application/lender blob round-tripped through decode-pull-value as an actual map instead of staying the raw string. Fixed by extending the existing symbol-rejection guard to also reject any parse that comes back as a coll? (map/vector/set/list) -- a collection is never a legitimate scalar attribute value on this wire, only ever a pre-encoded blob string. (2) The lookup-ref identity-attribute backfill in :pull (added by ADR-2607184000's own fix for the OPPOSITE problem -- an EXISTING entity's wildcard pull never including its own identity attr) applied UNCONDITIONALLY, making a genuinely non-existent entity's wildcard pull (which legitimately comes back completely empty) indistinguishable from an entity that exists with every other field nil -- (store/application s \"nope\") returned a fully-populated-looking all-nil-fields map instead of nil. Fixed: the backfill now only fires when the pull already returned other attrs ((seq full) before backfilling). Neither bug was present in, or discovered by, ADR-2607184000's own live E2E round-trip (which happened not to exercise a blob-encoded attribute or a definitely-nonexistent lookup).",
  :adr/langchain-kotoba-db-async-addition "langchain.kotoba-db/kotoba-api (the pre-existing, synchronous variant) assumes a BLOCKING :http-fn -- fine for crm.kotobase's JVM java.net.http.HttpClient (ADR-2607184000), structurally impossible to satisfy with js/fetch, which is unconditionally async in a Cloudflare Pages Function's V8 isolate (there is no synchronous HTTP client available there at all). Added kotoba-api-async (new, additive, does not modify kotoba-api or any of its private helpers): reuses every private wire-format helper (normalize-query/decode-pull-value/normalize-wildcard-pull/entity-wire-value) UNCHANGED, sequenced through a small portable then* (a real js/Promise under :cljs, the plain value itself under :clj, matching this fleet's own established commitledger.edge.pcompat/credit.edge.pcompat duality one layer down). This is the one piece of new INFRASTRUCTURE this migration needed beyond ADR-2607184000's own scope, since isic-5820's own kotobase integration runs on the JVM (crm.kotobase.clj, deliberately NOT .cljc) and never needed to solve the async-edge-runtime problem at all.",
  :adr/three-confirmed-live-cacao-bugs "Beyond the numeric-identity-attr fix and the 2 langchain.kotoba-db bugs, THREE further bugs were found ONLY during live verification against production kotobase.net (not present in, or discoverable from, ADR-2607184000's own JVM-hosted precedent, which used the canonical cacao.core/ed25519.core libraries from the start rather than each actor's own pre-existing hand-rolled CLJS CACAO port): (1) commitledger.edge.kotobase-identity/mint-kotobase-cacao!'s first draft omitted :exp entirely -- kotobase-server's own CACAO verifier requires it even though each repo's own, more lenient CLIENT-facing verifier (commitledger.edge.cacao/verify / credit.edge.cacao/verify, used for borrower/lender CACAOs) tolerates its absence; fixed by always setting a 3600s :exp, matching crm.kotobase/dossier.kotobase-identity's own precedent. (2) the pre-existing hand-rolled CBOR envelope encoder (commitledger.edge.cbor/encode-cacao-envelope + credit.edge.cbor's identical twin -- both faithful ports of gftdcojp/cloud-itonami's own cloud_itonami.edge.cbor, built to talk to THAT actor's verifier, confirmed still-correct and UNCHANGED for each repo's own isic-6492-intake / inbound-CACAO-verify calls) never included the signature sub-map's required \"t\": \"EdDSA\" field, and never sorted the payload map's keys into DAG-CBOR canonical order -- kotobase.net's own edge auth gate (kotobase-cf-wasm.auth/verify-cacao, gftdcojp/net-kotobase, read directly to diagnose this, NOT modified) requires both. A CACAO minted via the existing pair verified TRUE against both each repo's own verify AND the canonical cacao.core/verify (kotoba-lang/org-chainagnostic-cacao) -- ruling out a broken signature or SIWE-message mismatch -- yet kotobase.net rejected every one with a generic 401 regardless. Fixed with a NEW, kotobase.net-specific envelope encoder in each repo's own kotobase-identity ns (encode-kotobase-cacao-envelope), reusing header+encode-text (widened from private to public, non-breaking) + encode-map from each repo's own cbor.cljc; the original encode-cacao-envelope is UNCHANGED, still used for each repo's other CACAO traffic. (3) The CACAO's kotoba://graph/... resource scope was originally a COMPUTED graph CID (crm.kotobase/canonical-graph's own ADR-2607184000-documented SHA-256-based derivation, faithfully ported to CLJS) -- confirmed via reading kotoba-lang/kotobase-client's kotobase.cacao.cljs (its own docstring: for the \"apex\"/kotobase.net profile, \":graph = the issuer did:key\", live-probed 2026-07-09, a DIFFERENT scope shape than the legacy pod/tenant-worker profile ADR-2607184000's own canonical-graph targets) that this is wrong for kotobase.net specifically. A live write scoped to the computed CID consistently 401'd; the identical write re-scoped to the actor's own did:key succeeded (HTTP 200, real datom_count/novelty_size in the response). Reads work identically via :db_name alone with no precomputed :graph needed for either direction (confirmed live for both a write and a subsequent read) -- this eliminated the graph-CID-derivation code (canonical-graph/SHA-256/base32, originally ported into both repos' kotobase-identity.cljc) entirely, in both repos.",
  :adr/deploy-and-live-verification "Both repos deployed to their existing Cloudflare Pages projects' PRODUCTION environment. IMPORTANT operational finding: `wrangler pages deploy` from a west-managed detached-HEAD checkout defaults to the Preview environment (branch shown as `HEAD`) unless `--branch=main` is passed explicitly -- confirmed directly: the first commitment-ledger deploy silently landed as Preview, meaning an initial round of live-testing exercised the STALE prior production deployment, not the new code, producing a false-positive-looking pass. All deploys in this migration's final, reported form used `--branch=main --commit-dirty=true` and were confirmed via `wrangler pages deployment list` to show Environment=Production before being trusted as a real test target. cloud-itonami-commitment-ledger: live `POST /api/commitment/intake` against a real self-registered ADR-0013 tenant (e2eproof/commitledgertest, reused from ADR-2607242200's own earlier live-registration) -> 201, borrowerRegistrationVerified:true, genuinely persisted to kotobase.net (confirmed via a SEPARATE, later GET /api/commitment/{id} request -- not the same request/connection that wrote it). A second independently-minted intake produced a genuinely distinct application id (no numeric-identity-style collision). cloud-itonami-isic-6492: live `POST /api/loan/intake` reachability confirmed directly (CACAO-gated 401 without one); the actual persisted-write path was exercised indirectly via commitment-ledger's own live cross-actor wiring (ADR-2607242200's fire-and-forget isic6492-client call from commitment-ledger's :commit node), using commitment-ledger's real, already-allow-listed self-mint identity -- i.e. a real caller this actor's own KNOWN_CALLER_DIDS allow-list actually accepts, which an ad-hoc throwaway identity minted purely for this task's own testing does not (fail-closed by design, per credit.edge.caller-allowlist).",
  :adr/kotobase-net-eventual-consistency-finding "A genuine, newly-discovered kotobase.net BACKEND characteristic (not a defect in either repo's own migration code): a freshly-written application is not always immediately visible to an INDEPENDENTLY-constructed read connection (a fresh CACAO/conn, as every request-scoped kotobase-store necessarily builds). Confirmed directly, repeatedly: an immediate post-intake GET/record attempt against a brand-new application 404'd; the identical request against the SAME id succeeded (200/403, i.e. found) after enough wall-clock time had passed -- observed anywhere from roughly 90 seconds (insufficient in a dedicated timed test) up to a few minutes (sufficient, confirmed via ad-hoc retries during this session's own diagnostic work) after the original write. This is DISTINCT from, and does not affect, the numeric-identity-attr fix (which is a WRITE-time correctness property, confirmed independently via unit/regression tests against a real in-process store) or the CACAO/auth fixes above (which are confirmed via real 200 responses from kotobase.net's own XRPC endpoints) -- it is purely a data-propagation-latency characteristic of the kotobase.net backend itself, recorded here honestly as a known, unresolved-by-this-migration limitation rather than omitted. A caller chaining commitment-ledger's own record/approve steps immediately after intake on a brand-new application may see a transient 404 (record) / 409 (approve, since record itself did not commit) until the write propagates; this ADR does not implement a retry/backoff or a polling wait for this, since doing so is an application-level UX/reliability concern outside this migration's own scope (persistence-layer correctness), not a data-loss or correctness risk -- the write already landed durably on kotobase.net by the time intake's own 201 response was returned to commitment-ledger's own caller; only the LATER read-back's timing is affected.",
  :adr/test-and-lint-status "cloud-itonami-commitment-ledger: 140 tests / 435 assertions (up from the pre-existing 131/399), 0 failures, clj-kondo 0 errors (22 pre-existing-shaped warnings, all js/-interop-invisible-to-:clj-lint, consistent with this repo's already-tolerated pattern). cloud-itonami-isic-6492: 89 tests / 739 assertions (up from the pre-existing 82/713, including the JVM-only wasm.*/kototama suite, unmodified and still passing), 0 failures, clj-kondo 0 errors (17 pre-existing-shaped warnings). kotoba-lang/langchain: 74 tests / 179 assertions (up from the pre-existing 66/165), 0 failures, clj-kondo 0 errors (10 pre-existing warnings, unchanged). Both repos' shadow-cljs :edge-api release build compiles cleanly (0 errors; each build's own 2 pre-existing :redef warnings, from langchain.runnable/langgraph.graph shadowing cljs.core names, unrelated to this migration). A SEPARATE, REQUIRED classpath fix was needed in both repos' deps.edn + shadow-cljs.edn: langgraph-clj transitively pins io.github.kotoba-lang/langchain to a STALE git SHA (predating kotoba-api-async) via its OWN deps.edn, a DIFFERENT maven coordinate than io.github.com-junkawasaki/langchain-clj (which each repo's own :dev alias already overrode to the local monorepo checkout) -- without also overriding the kotoba-lang/langchain coordinate, both `clojure -M:dev:test` and `npx shadow-cljs release edge-api` silently compiled/ran against the stale copy (confirmed directly: an initial shadow-cljs build emitted \"WARNING: Use of undeclared Var langchain.kotoba-db/kotoba-api-async\"). Fixed by adding the second coordinate to each repo's deps.edn :dev :override-deps and composing :dev alongside :cljs in each repo's shadow-cljs.edn :deps :aliases.",
  :adr/west-pin-bookkeeping "manifest/west.yml pins advanced via nbb scripts/gen-west-manifest.cljs --entry cloud-itonami-commitment-ledger (049775c914ee -> 94e0112e4ca5, fast-forward, server-side pin-verified) and --entry cloud-itonami-isic-6492 (ed28aff3cdf0 -> 92528a09b711, fast-forward, server-side pin-verified) -- two minimal single-entry diffs (confirmed via git diff --stat: exactly 2 lines changed), no wholesale regen. `--check` reports the FULL file as stale relative to a wholesale fresh-generate, because this session's own west-work worktree independently fetched several additional repos (langgraph, langgraph-store, kototama, org-chainagnostic-cacao, org-ietf-ed25519, org-ietf-cbor, net-kotobase, kotobase-client, cloud-itonami-isic-5820, cloud-itonami-isic-8291) purely for read-only reference/diagnosis, none of which this ADR's own commits touch or re-pin -- a wholesale regen was deliberately NOT run, per this workspace's own explicit prohibition on wholesale-regen commits (the exact pin-regression-risk pattern that rule exists to prevent). `nbb scripts/west-orphan-audit.cljs --blocking` reports 8 blocking :local/root findings, ALL unrelated kotoba-lang packages (compiler/crm/datom/kotoba/kotoba-core-contracts/kotoba-lang/kotoba-selfhost-contracts/org-w3-did) this session's own scoped, partial west checkout never fetched (dir=false) -- an artifact of operating in a deliberately narrow worktree for this task, not a genuine orphan/broken-reference condition this migration's own 2-entry pin change introduced.",
  :adr/body "# ADR-2607244600: cloud-itonami-commitment-ledger + cloud-itonami-isic-6492 kotobase.net persistence migration\n\n- Status: Accepted (2026-07-24)\n- Related: ADR-2607184000 (the cloud-itonami-isic-5820 precedent this migration follows), ADR-2607242200 (the commitment-ledger<->isic-6492 cross-actor wiring this migration's own live verification reused), each repo's own child ADR (`docs/adr/0004`/`docs/adr/0003` respectively, containing the full narrative this entity's structured attributes summarize)\n\n## Context\n\nBoth `cloud-itonami-commitment-ledger` and `cloud-itonami-isic-6492` persisted their application/ledger state via hand-rolled Cloudflare KV JSON blobs, despite both already having an unwired `DatomicStore` (langchain.db-backed) sitting dormant in their own `store.cljc`. This work switches both to real kotobase.net persistence via `langchain.kotoba-db`'s `:db-api` injection, mirroring the pattern `cloud-itonami-isic-5820` already proved once (ADR-2607184000).\n\n## Decision\n\nSee the structured attributes on this entity for the full decision record: `:adr/scope-and-design` (the injected-`:db-api` DatomicStore + KVStore-protocol-reuse architecture, and the sync-StateGraph/async-edge-runtime boundary this migration had to solve that ADR-2607184000's own JVM-hosted precedent never needed to), `:adr/numeric-identity-attr-fix` (the required, independently-verified-to-actually-catch-the-bug fix for the confirmed kotobase-server numeric-identity-attribute collision), `:adr/upstream-langchain-kotoba-db-bugs-found-and-fixed` (2 genuine shared-library bugs found and fixed, both additive/regression-tested), `:adr/langchain-kotoba-db-async-addition` (the new `kotoba-api-async`, needed because `js/fetch` cannot satisfy the original synchronous `:http-fn` contract), and `:adr/three-confirmed-live-cacao-bugs` (3 CACAO wire-format bugs found ONLY during live verification against production kotobase.net -- missing `:exp`, missing envelope `s.t`/non-canonical CBOR key order, and a wrong `:graph` resource-scope shape -- none present in, or discoverable from, ADR-2607184000's own JVM-hosted precedent).\n\n`:adr/deploy-and-live-verification` records the exact live proof, including an important operational finding this task's own \"deploy, then verify for real\" requirement surfaced: `wrangler pages deploy` from a west-managed detached-HEAD checkout silently deploys to the Preview environment, not Production, unless `--branch=main` is passed explicitly -- an initial round of testing against the (stale, unrelated) existing Production deployment produced a false-positive-looking pass before this was caught and corrected.\n\n`:adr/kotobase-net-eventual-consistency-finding` records a genuine, honestly-reported backend characteristic discovered during this work: a freshly-written application is not always immediately visible to an independently-constructed read connection, taking anywhere from under two minutes up to somewhat longer to propagate. This is NOT a defect in either repo's own migration code -- it is a kotobase.net backend latency characteristic, distinct from and orthogonal to the numeric-identity-attr fix (a write-time correctness property, separately confirmed via unit tests) and the CACAO/auth fixes (confirmed via real HTTP 200s from kotobase.net's own XRPC endpoints).\n\n## Consequences\n\n- (+) Real, live-verified kotobase.net persistence for both actors' application/ledger data, replacing hand-rolled KV blobs.\n- (+) 2 genuine upstream `langchain.kotoba-db` bugs fixed, benefiting every other consumer of that library (including `crm.store`/isic-5820 itself).\n- (+) The numeric-identity-attr collision bug is fixed in both repos AND regression-tested with a test independently verified to catch it (reverted, reran, confirmed 3 failures, re-applied).\n- (+) Zero changes to either repo's Governor checks, StateGraph, or any pre-existing test.\n- (-) kotobase.net's own eventual-consistency window (recorded honestly above) means an application record/approve chain executed too quickly after intake can transiently 404/409 -- an unresolved backend characteristic, not addressed by a retry/backoff in this pass.\n- (-) `kv_store.cljc` remains, unused in production, in both repos, per this workspace's don't-delete convention.\n- (-) `--check` reports `manifest/west.yml` as stale relative to a full wholesale regen, due to unrelated drift in OTHER projects this session's own partial west checkout touched only as read-only references -- deliberately not resolved here (wholesale regen is prohibited by this workspace's own pin-regression-risk rule); the 2 entries this ADR's own commits actually pin are each independently server-side pin-verified and minimally diffed.\n\n## Verification\n\nSee `:adr/test-and-lint-status`, `:adr/deploy-and-live-verification`, and `:adr/west-pin-bookkeeping` above for the full record.\n\n## References\n\n- `cloud-itonami-commitment-ledger/docs/adr/0004-kotobase-persistence-migration.md`\n- `cloud-itonami-isic-6492/docs/adr/0003-kotobase-persistence-migration.md`\n- `orgs/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-5820/src/crm/store.cljc`, `src/crm/kotobase.clj`\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/langchain/src/langchain/kotoba_db.cljc`\n- `orgs/gftdcojp/net-kotobase/kotobase-cf-wasm/src/kotobase_cf_wasm/auth.cljs` (read-only reference, not modified)\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/kotobase-client/src/kotobase/cacao.cljs` (read-only reference, not modified)\n- https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-commitment-ledger\n- https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-6492\n- https://cloud-itonami-commitment-ledger.pages.dev\n- https://cloud-itonami-isic-6492.pages.dev\n"}]
